  "scope": "Both ladder collapse angles sampled every three movie frames through 1.45 seconds, plus 1.983/2.983/3.483-second settled samples. Peak-neighbor native full-resolution frames 3, 4, 5 inspected for each view. This is sampled collapse visual review, not an every-physics-tick visual review.",
  "findings": [
    {
      "phase": "release peak",
      "finding": "The 82.9037 rad/s left-hand transient occurs at release +0.0667 seconds. Adjacent full-resolution renders at +0.05, +0.067 and +0.083 seconds retain the wrist connection; the fast local turn does not show a gross detached snap or complete hand spin."
    },
    {
      "phase": "0.0 to 0.85 seconds",
      "finding": "Hands release from the rungs as the body descends and folds. Limbs remain attached and retain recognizable upper/forearm and thigh/shin segments; the rear oblique angle follows the complete fall to the floor."
    },
    {
      "phase": "0.9 to 3.483 seconds",
      "finding": "After impact the body turns into a prone resting pose, arms settle forward and no continuing explosive motion appears. The rear oblique camera contains the complete head, hands and boots throughout settling."
    },
    {
      "phase": "view limitation",
      "finding": "The front grip angle clearly shows the fingers, rungs and early wrist release. Its landing view is partly occluded by the platform, so full-body landing and settled pose were assessed from the clear rear oblique view."
    }
  ],
